.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt PROCESS 7
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm process
.Nd processes, starting programs, waiting, and parentage on ABSD
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A process is an address space, a table of handles
.Pq Xr handles 7 ,
a PID, a parent, and exactly one task: there are no threads.
PIDs are not reused within a boot; process table slots are.
There are sixteen slots in the whole system.
.Ss Starting a program
A new program is always a new process.
There is no
.Fn fork
and no
.Fn exec
that replaces a running program.
To run a program from the filesystem, the parent reads the whole file with
its own filesystem authority and hands the bytes to the kernel, which checks
and loads them into a new process
.Pq Fn spawn_image .
The file must be a static x86-64 ELF executable of at most 32 MiB: no
dynamic linking, no interpreter, no
.Ql #!
scripts.
There is no execute permission; what stands in for it is the
.Em spawn right ,
a per-process attribute without which every start is refused.
Nothing restores it once withheld.
.Pp
The child starts with an ordered set of handles copied from the parent,
never with more rights than the parent's copy, and a startup record on its
first handle carrying its arguments, environment, working directory, and
program name.
Its standard input, output, and error are kernel pipes (or the parent's own
streams, copied); there are no file descriptors.
Everything else about its authority is in
.Xr handles 7 .
.Ss Waiting and killing
Starting a child gives the parent a process handle with the wait and kill
rights.
With it the parent can wait for the child to end, which collects its
outcome and frees its slot (reaping), or kill it: the child ends at once,
wherever it was blocked, and everything it held is released.
A process ends in one of three ways: it exits with a status, it faults
(the kernel records the vector and address), or it is killed.
In a shell, a fault or kill is reported as status 128.
.Pp
There are no signals.
Nothing interrupts a process from outside except its parent's kill.
^C on a session's terminal is not delivered to any process: the SSH server
tells the session's shell on a control channel of its own, and the shell,
the parent, kills its running commands; see
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
A write to a pipe whose readers are all gone fails with a broken-pipe
error; it does not stop the writer.
.Ss Parentage
The parent is the process that started the child; nothing else is.
When a process ends, its children are reparented to init (orphan adoption).
Adoption grants init no handle and no authority over them; it lets init
reap them when they end.
There is no process-tree kill: ending a process does not end its children.
.Pp
In an SSH session the tree is:
.Nm sshd
starts the session's shell (or, for
.Ql ssh host command ,
that command); the shell starts the commands you type.
The shell keeps the spawn right; the commands it starts do not, so they
cannot start programs, and a
.Ql ssh host command
without shell syntax gets none either.
The tree is at most two deep: the SSH server holds a kill right on the
shell, the shell on each of its commands.
.Pp
When the session ends (the client disconnects, its idle timeout passes, or
the system is going down),
.Nm sshd
tells the shell on the session's control channel first; the shell kills
and reaps its commands and exits.
Only if it has not exited within a second of reading that, or has not
read it within 10 seconds, does
.Nm sshd
kill the shell itself
.Pq Ql SSH SESSION END ... forced=true ;
its commands would then be adopted by init and run to their own end, with
their standard input at end of file, their writes to the terminal failing,
and the file server refusing their channel, which stays out of use until
they end.
Init has no right to end them, and a
.Xr shutdown 8
or
.Xr reboot 8
that finds one still running stops the machine instead.
A command
.Nm sshd
started directly (no shell) is killed and reaped at once.
.Ss What a process can see
A process can inspect itself and, through the process handles it holds, its
own children, and nothing else.
There is no list of all processes.
A session's listing,
.Xr ps 1 ,
is the SSH server's answer for that session alone: it names the session's
shell, which it holds a handle to, and counts what else holds the
session's input and filesystem channels.
There is no
.Nm kill
command: only a parent ends a child.
.Ss Replacement is not recovery
Starting a new instance of a program after an earlier one ended is a
restart, and the new instance is a replacement.
A replacement is not a recovery: recovery is claimed only once the
replacement has read the state the earlier instance left and reconciled it.
.Pp
In the qualified boot nothing is restarted.
If the SSH server ends, or the file server ends while the SSH server runs,
the boot ends and is reported failed.
A policy for replacing either in a long-running boot is not defined yet.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7 ,
.Xr handles 7
.Pp
In the kernel repository:
.Pa docs/PROCESS-STARTUP.md
(the start contract and the startup record) and
.Pa docs/TERMINOLOGY.md .
.Sh CAVEATS
If the SSH server itself ends while sessions run, nothing tells their
shells to end: what they run is left to init, and the boot ends failed
instead of restarting (see
.Sx Replacement is not recovery
and
.Xr shutdown 8 ) .
Sixteen processes are few: init, the file server, and the SSH server take
three, and each open shell running a pipeline takes one per command.
